# Project Overview

## Motivation

Project 1400 began with a simple but ambitious question:

> Can a computer generate a believable political map for the entire world without manually designing every province?

Most strategy games rely on hand-crafted maps created through thousands of hours of manual work. While this approach offers complete artistic control, it is difficult to reproduce, difficult to modify and almost impossible to scale.

This project explores a different idea: instead of designing the map itself, design the system that generates it.

The objective is to create a reproducible geospatial pipeline capable of transforming real-world geographic and historical data into a coherent political map suitable for a grand strategy game set around the year 1400.

---

## Vision

Project 1400 is not intended to replace human map design.

Instead, it aims to demonstrate that procedural generation can produce results that are geographically coherent, historically plausible and visually convincing while remaining fully reproducible.

Rather than relying on handcrafted regional adjustments, the system attempts to apply the same generation principles across the entire world. Whether processing the Iberian Peninsula, the Himalayas, the Amazon basin or the Indonesian archipelago, the objective is to preserve consistent quality using a common methodology.

---

## Objectives

The project has five primary objectives:

- Generate approximately 32,000 coherent territorial units from a global geographic dataset.
- Produce provinces that follow natural geographic structures instead of arbitrary geometric partitions.
- Incorporate historical and environmental information into the generation process.
- Maintain visual consistency across regions with radically different geography.
- Keep the entire pipeline deterministic, reproducible and open to continuous refinement.

---

## Scope

The project focuses exclusively on territorial generation.

It does not attempt to simulate politics, economics, warfare or diplomacy. Those systems belong to the game itself.

Its responsibility is much narrower:

To generate a world map that provides a strong geographical foundation upon which those systems could later operate.

---

## Methodology

Rather than generating provinces directly, the project follows a multi-stage pipeline.

Raw geographic information is progressively transformed into higher-level representations through successive processing stages. Each stage enriches the available information and prepares the data for the following one, allowing complex territorial decisions to emerge from the interaction of multiple geographic and historical factors.

This modular approach makes the system easier to analyse, validate and improve over time.

---

## Design Philosophy

Several principles guide every design decision throughout the project.

The system should explain *why* a province exists, not simply *where* it appears.

Geography should influence borders without determining them mechanically.

Historical plausibility should emerge from data rather than subjective interpretation whenever possible.

Local improvements should never reduce global consistency.

Most importantly, every decision should favour reproducibility over manual intervention. If a result cannot be generated again from the same inputs, it is considered a flaw in the design rather than a feature.

---

## Current Status

The core generation pipeline has been implemented and tested across multiple regions of the world.

Large portions of Europe, North Africa, the Middle East and Asia have already been used as validation areas to refine the scoring functions, territorial growth rules and quality metrics.

Development is currently focused on improving global consistency, reducing edge cases and expanding the validation process before the methodology is applied to the complete world.

---

## Why this project?

Project 1400 is ultimately less about producing a map than about understanding how complex geographic systems can be modelled algorithmically.

It combines geographic information systems (GIS), historical research, procedural generation and iterative engineering into a single long-term experiment whose primary goal is not automation itself, but the design of a transparent, reproducible and explainable territorial generation system.
